---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/russell-decisive/30min';

const title = 'Finance for technology companies | Decisive';
const description =
  'A sale price is a multiple times earnings. We grow the earnings behind your revenue and make them believable to a buyer, in 14 days.';

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Mental Accounting',
    body: 'A headline growth number hides which customers and products actually make money. A buyer runs the segment math you never did, and prices the cross-subsidy back to you.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Certainty Illusion',
    body: 'A dashboard full of green metrics can feel like proof the business is healthy. A buyer tests the profit behind the growth. The numbers that stopped meaning money become the re-trade.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Loss Aversion',
    body: 'The marquee customer you are most afraid to reprice or lose is often the concentration a buyer discounts hardest. Naming it early is worth real multiple.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">For owner-led technology companies</span>
      <h1>
        <span class="h1-line">You can't sell a tech company on numbers</span>
        <span class="h1-line h1-line-accent">a buyer can't trust.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-subhead">
        A sale price is a multiple times earnings. <span class="dollar">We work both numbers:</span> we grow the earnings behind your revenue, and we make them believable to a buyer. Growth hides which customers and products actually pay.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-microcopy">30 minutes with Russ. No pitch. You leave knowing what a buyer would re-price, and what you could recover.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">What a buyer re-prices in a tech company</span>
        <h2>Revenue growth is easy to show. Profit a buyer believes is not.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A buyer of a technology company tests gross-margin quality, revenue recognition, customer concentration, retention economics, and how much of the business is the founder. Every soft answer is a discount, and every one is also earnings you can grow before you go to market.
        </p>
        <div class="category-pull">
          Accurate and ugly beats polished and untrustworthy. Buyers know the difference.
        </div>
        <p>
          The mess isn't negligence. Growth covered it, and the people producing the numbers were never paid to look. Everyone who touches your books closes the month. Nobody is making your company worth believing, and that is the job we do. The day a sale, a PE approach, or a succession comes into view, every soft number converts from a chronic annoyance into priced risk, and the clock starts.
        </p>
        <div class="delay-callout">
          The trailing-twelve clock doesn't start until the numbers are true. Every month you wait moves the sale date a month.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="traps">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Where the value is trapped</div>
      <h2>Three traps behind a growth chart.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.88);">
        The Six Trap Diagnostic&trade; reads your numbers the way a buyer will. Three of the six do the most damage in a technology company. The patterns are structural, not failures of the owner.
      </p>
      <div class="trap-grid">
        {traps.map((t, i) => (
          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-num">Trap {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            <div class="trap-name">{t.name}</div>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="traps-footer">
        We name the stack, price each one two ways, what a buyer discounts and what you can recover, and turn the read into a plan. We don't audit the past. We make the company worth believing.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Proof</span>
        <h2>The mechanism reads the same across industries, because the traps do.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="worked">
        <span class="label">Professional services, $30M revenue</span>
        <h4>A clean close is not a fast one, and neither tells a buyer where the cash is stuck.</h4>
        <p>
          Cash was trapped in a billing and close cadence nobody had revisited, and clean-looking reports masked the leak. We rebuilt the rhythm, pulled the working capital forward, and put margin on a weekly line of sight, the kind of report a buyer believes. Recurring-revenue businesses run the same play.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Working capital recovered</span><span class="v accent">$380,000</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Close time</span><span class="v">22 to 3 days</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Margin improvement</span><span class="v">~$50,000 / mo</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 22px;">
        Anonymized by segment and revenue band until attribution is confirmed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Tiers and pricing</span>
        <h2>Find. Install. Run.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most owners start with the 14-day Sellable-Numbers Scan and move into the 90-Day Resolution. Pricing is banded by revenue, and the floor is $5M.
        </p>
        <p class="promise-line">
          The Scan promise: at least 3x the fee in owner-accepted value identified by Day 14, or you pay nothing.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta-row" style="margin-top: 8px;">
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/#tiers">See tiers and pricing</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guarantee">Read the guarantee</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="close-section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">Book a fit call</span>
      <h2>Decide from the numbers. Prove it in the cash.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        In 14 days you see your company the way a buyer will, in dollars, on both sides of the equation. You leave with the re-price read, the worth read, and the first three moves, whether or not we ever work together again. It starts with one 30-minute call.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-microcopy { font-size: 15px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7); margin: 10px 0 0; }
  .promise-line { font-family: Inter, sans-serif; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
